WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.843
(upbeat music)

00:00:09.421 --> 00:00:10.965
- [Brent] What number are you on?

00:00:10.965 --> 00:00:13.465
(car revving)

00:00:18.944 --> 00:00:21.527
(upbeat music)

00:00:45.486 --> 00:00:46.592
- Yo. What's up?

00:00:46.592 --> 00:00:47.425
- What's going on?

00:00:47.425 --> 00:00:49.180
- Tell us, tell us about your place.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.525
- Well we do whole hog barbecue here.

00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:52.583
That's what makes us special.

00:00:52.583 --> 00:00:54.042
And we cook it with just wood.

00:00:54.042 --> 00:00:56.117
There's no gas in here, only smokers.

00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:59.024
It takes about 12 to 15
hours to cook a pig that way.

00:00:59.024 --> 00:01:02.621
We cook the pig until it's an
internal temperature of 193.

00:01:02.621 --> 00:01:04.967
All that fatty goodness
just starts to melt at that

00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:07.567
temperature and then it just
kind of melts and just pours

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.097
through the meat and the meat
instantly changes texture.

00:01:11.097 --> 00:01:13.271
This is a sauce that
we just started making.

00:01:13.271 --> 00:01:14.857
I'm calling it XX Red.

00:01:14.857 --> 00:01:16.237
It's just a spicy red sauce.

00:01:16.237 --> 00:01:18.113
Tomato based, there is some vinegar.

00:01:18.113 --> 00:01:19.907
We cook it underneath
the pig and it's just

00:01:19.907 --> 00:01:21.784
getting dripped on and getting a layer

00:01:21.784 --> 00:01:23.922
of smoke from that bottom of the pit.

00:01:23.922 --> 00:01:25.755
- Wow. That's not bad.

00:01:26.648 --> 00:01:29.231
(upbeat music)

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:36.894
(car revving)

00:01:36.894 --> 00:01:39.477
(upbeat music)

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.133
Where are we?

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:54.002
- We're at Lexington Barbecue,
Lexington, North Carolina.

00:01:54.002 --> 00:01:55.658
- What are you guys known for?

00:01:55.658 --> 00:01:57.614
- We're known for smoking pork shoulder.

00:01:57.614 --> 00:01:58.741
All we do is salt on it.

00:01:58.741 --> 00:02:00.939
We don't baste it, we don't inject it,

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:02.858
we don't put any sauce
on it until you order it.

00:02:02.858 --> 00:02:04.146
- What am I about to eat, like

00:02:04.146 --> 00:02:05.419
explain to me what's on my plate.

00:02:05.419 --> 00:02:07.688
- Original barbecue plate
always comes with a 'slaw,

00:02:07.688 --> 00:02:10.218
chopped barbecue, french
fries, hush puppies also.

00:02:10.218 --> 00:02:11.460
- How long you guys been around?

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.232
- Grandpa started at 1962.

00:02:14.232 --> 00:02:16.026
Used to be a gas station
and a radio station.

00:02:16.026 --> 00:02:17.762
I think it had two tables in it.

00:02:17.762 --> 00:02:19.850
But ever since then we're
selling on an average week

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.438
we'll feed 7,000 people a week right now.

00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:23.827
Yeah I mean nothing's changed, really.

00:02:23.827 --> 00:02:26.462
We still fire the pits the same way,

00:02:26.462 --> 00:02:28.462
the same amount of time.

00:02:30.786 --> 00:02:31.619
- Hell yeah.

00:02:31.619 --> 00:02:33.030
- Love that.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.928
(car engine starting)

00:02:36.928 --> 00:02:39.511
(upbeat music)

00:03:06.835 --> 00:03:08.235
- My name's Sam Jones.

00:03:08.235 --> 00:03:10.022
Welcome to Skylight Inn Barbecue.

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:13.646
So our family started
Skylight the summer of 1947.

00:03:13.646 --> 00:03:16.176
This year actually makes
70 years that we've

00:03:16.176 --> 00:03:18.976
been doing the same
thing every single day.

00:03:18.976 --> 00:03:21.052
My grandfather opened at age 19.

00:03:21.052 --> 00:03:22.636
He had a third grade education.

00:03:22.636 --> 00:03:25.260
One of the smartest men
you ever meet in your life.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.617
The staple is whole hog
pork chopped by hand

00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:30.891
that was cooked for 18
hours, lightly seasoned,

00:03:30.891 --> 00:03:33.803
topped with a piece of
cornbread that the recipe

00:03:33.803 --> 00:03:37.653
has been the same for almost
100 years with a coleslaw

00:03:37.653 --> 00:03:41.678
recipe sitting on top of that
that's 70 plus years old.

00:03:41.678 --> 00:03:44.369
That's not just a paper
boat with pork in it.

00:03:44.369 --> 00:03:47.219
That's a piece of North Carolina history.

00:03:47.219 --> 00:03:48.392
- Actually, we didn't talk about this.

00:03:48.392 --> 00:03:50.311
Oh. Damn, I'm so sorry about that.

00:03:50.311 --> 00:03:52.154
- And this sauce that he
just poured on my pants.

00:03:52.154 --> 00:03:53.937
(laughter)

00:03:53.937 --> 00:03:56.171
It's a thin sauce that comes
out real easy on your jeans.

00:03:56.171 --> 00:03:58.618
Barbecue shouldn't be defined by sauce.

00:03:58.618 --> 00:04:01.737
It should be defined by how
it's prepared and what it is.

00:04:01.737 --> 00:04:03.439
In Aden, North Carolina, whole hog

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:07.002
wood fire barbecue is what barbecue is.

00:04:07.002 --> 00:04:08.658
- Thank you so much, Sam.

00:04:08.658 --> 00:04:10.201
- Sam Jones university.

00:04:10.201 --> 00:04:12.029
(laughter)

00:04:12.029 --> 00:04:14.612
(upbeat music)

00:04:29.201 --> 00:04:30.034
- Started on the corner in '72.

00:04:30.034 --> 00:04:31.701
We didn't have much.

00:04:32.554 --> 00:04:35.274
Sam my brother-in-law, and my wife,

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:38.310
and Dominique is my grandson.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.000
I did a little bit of mechanic.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.702
- That's why you guys
wear mechanic shirts?

00:04:41.702 --> 00:04:43.185
- Yeah.

00:04:43.185 --> 00:04:47.568
We do a hog in the back
and do mechanics up front.

00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:50.834
We changed and went on to barbecue.

00:04:50.834 --> 00:04:52.880
- What's special about your barbecue?

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.018
- Sauce.

00:04:54.018 --> 00:04:54.851
- [Kwesi] The sauce?

00:04:54.851 --> 00:04:55.684
- [Roosevelt] Yeah.

00:04:55.684 --> 00:04:56.517
- [Kwesi] What's in the sauce?

00:04:56.517 --> 00:05:00.715
- [Roosevelt] Vinegar, pepper, that's it.

00:05:00.715 --> 00:05:04.395
Once you eat it one time,
you gonna come back.

00:05:04.395 --> 00:05:07.223
We have people from all over the world.

00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:08.958
- Did you say that you
eat this for breakfast?

00:05:08.958 --> 00:05:12.145
- Yeah. I eat it all the time.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.728
(upbeat music)

00:05:24.339 --> 00:05:26.961
(car engine revving)

00:05:26.961 --> 00:05:29.544
(upbeat music)

00:05:42.807 --> 00:05:43.790
- My name's Chris Bare.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.274
We're at Sweatman's Bar-B-Que
in Holly Hill, South Carolina.

00:05:46.274 --> 00:05:49.782
Serving whole hog barbecue
cooked over a real wood fire.

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.510
The restaurant proper
has been here since 1977.

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:54.766
Originally started and
founded by Bub Sweatman.

00:05:54.766 --> 00:05:56.942
He started cooking whole
hogs in his backyard,

00:05:56.942 --> 00:05:59.427
selling it out the back of
his pickup truck in the '50s.

00:05:59.427 --> 00:06:00.260
It became popular enough that he

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.046
decided to buy this old farmhouse.

00:06:02.046 --> 00:06:05.157
Mustard sauce is just, I
mean, really that's all it is.

00:06:05.157 --> 00:06:06.446
It's not a fancy barbecue sauce.

00:06:06.446 --> 00:06:07.699
I's just gonna be mustard,
little bit of sugar,

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:09.359
little bit of vinegar,
little bit of pepper.

00:06:09.359 --> 00:06:12.014
- So good. I honestly
can't stop eating it.

00:06:12.014 --> 00:06:13.961
- It's kind of a regional low
country South Carolina thing,

00:06:13.961 --> 00:06:18.009
but it's what Sweatman's has
done since its inception.

00:06:18.009 --> 00:06:19.538
Just compliments the pork perfectly.

00:06:19.538 --> 00:06:21.272
You get that little bit of
smoke, little bit of fat,

00:06:21.272 --> 00:06:22.914
then you get that sweetness and that tang.

00:06:22.914 --> 00:06:24.463
The mustard kind of cuts through how rich

00:06:24.463 --> 00:06:27.378
that meat is and it's
the perfect combination.

00:06:27.378 --> 00:06:28.590
- We appreciate it.

00:06:28.590 --> 00:06:29.423
Thank you so much.

00:06:29.423 --> 00:06:30.325
- Thank you guys for coming, man.

00:06:30.325 --> 00:06:31.510
I appreciate it.
- Can we get seconds?

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.009
- Yeah, go back man, go ahead.

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:34.353
- I think that's thirds, though.

00:06:34.353 --> 00:06:36.209
- Yeah, no that is thirds for him.

00:06:36.209 --> 00:06:39.796
- I just didn't want to leave, you know.

00:06:39.796 --> 00:06:42.379
(upbeat music)

00:06:58.471 --> 00:06:59.310
- [Brent] What is it that you guys

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.592
are doing here that makes you different?

00:07:00.592 --> 00:07:03.497
- Beef barbecue, which is
pretty much a Texas thing.

00:07:03.497 --> 00:07:07.600
Start off with certified Angus
beef, USDA prime, brisket.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:10.714
Put our dry rub on it then
we smoke it over all wood

00:07:10.714 --> 00:07:14.953
and offset indirect smokers
that we custom built ourselves

00:07:14.953 --> 00:07:17.177
and it takes about 18 to 20 hours

00:07:17.177 --> 00:07:18.604
to cook a brisket all the way through.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.365
So we've got the lean end
of the brisket down here.

00:07:20.365 --> 00:07:21.357
- Yum.

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:22.818
- And here's the fatty side over here.

00:07:22.818 --> 00:07:24.584
This lays over the top as the lean muscle.

00:07:24.584 --> 00:07:27.504
There's a little bit of burnt
ends from the fatty side.

00:07:27.504 --> 00:07:29.108
- You built the smokers
yourself with your bare hands?

00:07:29.108 --> 00:07:29.941
- Yeah.

00:07:29.941 --> 00:07:30.774
- You can weld?

00:07:30.774 --> 00:07:31.607
- Yeah.

00:07:31.607 --> 00:07:32.440
- Where'd you learn how to weld?

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:33.764
- Trying to figure out how
to make lighter cupids.

00:07:33.764 --> 00:07:34.842
- Wow.

00:07:34.842 --> 00:07:35.810
How long did that take?

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.152
- [John] A couple years.

00:07:37.152 --> 00:07:39.044
- [Brent] What was so shitty
about other barbecue pits

00:07:39.044 --> 00:07:40.440
that you were like I
have to make this one?

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.533
- Yeah. I made ones specifically
designed to cook brisket

00:07:43.533 --> 00:07:45.874
and that's after, you know,
years of trial and error.

00:07:45.874 --> 00:07:46.909
Cheers. Thanks guys.

00:07:46.909 --> 00:07:48.206
- Thank you.
- Glad to have you.

00:07:48.206 --> 00:07:50.789
(upbeat music)

